 held on a Sunday, he withdrew from the 100 metres. But this was fabricated. Liddell knew about the Olympic schedule several months in advance and never intended to run the 100 in Paris. He focused instead on the 200 and 400, adjusting his training for the longer sprints."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Besides Liddell, the other favored athlete in Paris in 1924 was the defending champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/78873"} "Charley Paddock"] ", of the United States. In the 100 final, Abrahams faced four Americans — Paddock, " [:a {:href "/athletes/79001"} "Jackson Scholz"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/78826"} "Loren Murchison"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/78134"} "Chet Bowman"] " — and New Zealander " [:a {:href "/athletes/74341"} "Arthur Porritt"] ". Abrahams won the race, as depicted in the movie, becoming the first European winner of the 100 metres. Scholz won the silver medal, with Porritt in third, and Paddock a well-beaten fifth."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/68824"} "Harold Abrahams"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {} "11.0 (1 h14)"] [:td {} "10.6 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "10.6 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "10.6 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/79001"} "Jackson Scholz"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "10.8 (1 h13)"] [:td {} "10.8 (1 h6)"] [:td {} "10.8 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "10.7 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]]